snelling@bnr.ca (Peter Snelling) writes:

>I just went into an armor store, and picked up some armor: boots and a 
>"faded pall" (I was a priest so I knew they weren't cursed).
>I had enough to pay for them, and no good way of robbing the shop,
>so I just payed for them.  I put them on, and went to leave, but the 
>shopkeeper wouldn't get out of the way.  I waited and waited but he 
>just wouldn't move.

>Finally I took off the faded pall, and he moved.  I put it back on
>and he stood in front on the door.  I took it off again and he moved.

>Anyone know what kind of armor this was?

>Wait a sec...

>I just figured it out... I left the store, put it on and tried to get 
>back in.  He said invisible customers aren't welcome.  So it was a 
>cloak of invisibility.  I wonder why I didn't turn invisible?  Maybe 
>I picked up the see invisible attribute somewhere.  Hmmm.

There is a potion of see-invisible, but I don't know if it's permanent.
The message will be "this tastes like (fruit) juice" or something very like
that.  I don't know the difference between real fruit juice and see invis.
messages.  Also, Elves can see invisible right off.

>Good thing it wasn't cursed.  Let this be a warning: Putting on a cursed
>cloak of invisibility in a shop could be a big pain.

It can also be good.  You can stand a few squares from the shopkeeper and
blast him with a wand or missile weapon.  He won't leave the door as long
as you are invisible.  Just make sure you don't run out of arrows or wand
charges...

